7.1  Load average

TL;DR
  - Load average: the number of tasks running or waiting for a CPU, plus those
    waiting on disk (D state), averaged over 1, 5 and 15 minutes.
  - Compare it with the number of CPUs: on 8 CPUs, a load of 8 means fully busy,
    and 16 means tasks are queuing.
  - A high load with idle CPUs points to I/O: a slow disk or network storage.

      uptime
      nproc

On your machine
  CPU       11th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i7-1165G7 @ 2.80GHz · 8 logical CPUs
  Load      1.25, 0.90, 0.62 over 1, 5 and 15 minutes, for 8 CPUs
  Tasks     2 running right now, 1043 in all
  Governor  powersave

Pitfalls
  - It's not a percentage: 2.0 overloads a 1-CPU machine and barely touches a
    32-CPU one.
  - Linux counts tasks waiting on disk, most other Unixes don't: the same work
    shows a higher load on Linux.
  - The values decay gradually, they aren't plain averages: the 1-minute value
    still carries part of older spikes.
  - To see what keeps the CPUs busy, run top and press 1 for one line per CPU.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 7.1 --why

Related
  Commands  uptime, nproc, top
  Paths     /proc/loadavg
  Hub       idk cpu CPU
  See also  idk 4.1 Process states and zombies
  See also  idk 4.3 nice and priorities
